es but still most  players probably won't care but i think  ubisoft knew this would happen as  everything in the game is rated by a  number and a higher obviously means  better so i'd normally just pick the  highest number and go on my way for the  events themselves they're actually quite  fun and really exhilarating there's  something about going 50 miles per hour  down a slope only to have it turn at the  end where one wrong move can send you  off the cliff that is just so fun the  races are where the majority of that  style of gameplay will take place and  it's strongly recommended from me at  least to not do tricks during the  regular race as you're not going to get  any points for it and you're just  setting yourself up for failure most of  the skill for this race comes down to  precision and speed being able to handle  the fast speeds while also moving around  obstacles is key and was very fun even  if most of the time i would wipe out for  the trick events i feel the same way  these are less intense as you're not  bound by other racers but by the number  of tricks you can perform in a specific  run the trick courses give you a set  amount of tricks you can perform as to  not cheat and do a hundred of them on  the way down so expending all of your  tricks too early will leave you with a  long drive of no points and possibly  missing out on the bigger ramps and  finishing the race with tricks left over  is a waste of potential points so it's  this combination of doing enough tricks  but not too many too early and also  doing the complex tricks that net more  points both the race and trig events  clearly show a skill curve that allows  the players who really know what they're  doing to succeed which means it makes  the game more competitive encouraging  you to get better yourself which then  leads to more playtime and ultimately  more fun with great rewards as now you  can pull off those bizarre tricks and  maneuvers you didn't think were possible  the last modes we haven't discussed yet  in detail are the mass races and the  competitive modes the mass races are  essentially ubisoft's magnum opus the  one thing that they've been advertising  to death about the game and as i  mentioned before they're 30 to 60 player  races that happen every hour i mean this  in no exaggeration this is the best  content in the entire game nothing in  this game could top how fun these were  it's the complete test of skill as it  goes through all the different sports  and forces you to be skillful in all of  them in the beginning though you could  be going strong in first until one  slight miscalculation brings you to the  back of the group but remember everyone  is just as susceptible to this as you  and now you could be in the front again  with a flawless performance and ending  on a strong finish the beauty of this  game mode is nothing can be used as an  excuse the game provides the players  with the exact same thing everyone knows  what different segments are a part of  the race ahead of time and everyone  wears the same clothes and equipment so  nothing is unfair if you end up in last  place it's purely on you and  alternatively if you end up in first  it's also purely under skill the races  are three rounds and depending on your  position at the end you'll receive some  points after all three rounds everyone's  points are totaled to showcase the final  ranking not only does this give you  bragging rights if you win but you also  gain lots of stars the highest i've ever  gotten is 7th place but that's still  quite impressive considering 29 other  people were racing against me regardless  it's the best content in the game and  some of the most fun i've had with this  game for the competitive modes you have  two options there is the free-for-all  section and the trick battle section  available at the home base free for all  is a 12-man race just like many of the  race events that you've been doing  except with real people and there's a  ranking system i'm assuming these races  are gonna go across all the sports mode  since i did play a bike and a ski race  so it's safe to assume that all races  will be included for the trick battles  currently it's just a 6v6 snow tricks  battle you get put on a team of six  people and have to perform different  tricks on various ramps and railings  when you perform a trick it gets lit up  in blue and the enemy is in red if one  team captures a whole section they get  five times the points for performing  tricks in that sector this was honestly  really fun and encourages sticking with  the team but also performing major  tricks and is a really cool way of  learning tricks if you haven't tried any  yet seeing his bikes to my knowledge  cannot grind on ledges or rails it seems  that this may be for snow sports only  however there's nothing stopping them  from adding a different arena more  tailored towards bikes for this game  mode after any of these matches if you  win you'll increase your division rank  and lose rank if you lose just like any  other competitive game mode there seems  to be an entry fee for these matches as  you're essentially wagering your  division points it seems that in  division 10 there is no fee but when you  get to division nine there's a fee of  five division points but at division  nine you do gain five points for losing  so it seems like the first couple  divisions there isn't a major penalty  for losing just yet although i assume  that would get more punishing the higher  you go up your total divisions points  get added to the weekly division level  on the left hand side you can then gain  more items in gear while you go higher  however this is where i'm a little  confused there's also a timer in the top  right this is most likely a timer that  resets the mode but what does it reset  the division ranks the weekly level the  game modes maybe all of them this sadly  is the one thing i fully don't  understand about the game but it doesn't  seem to be that big of a deal and  judging by the fact that both game modes  have separate locations at the main hub  this leads me to believe that there will  be multiple game modes per week because  i feel like leaving all this empty room  on screen just does not make sense as a  final note on the game modes i think  writer's republic does a good job with  them there is a lot of options that give  everyone at least something to enjoy and  an adequate amount of challenge to the  events that'll give you a reason to  continue playing and getting better at  the game the only thing we haven't  discussed yet in terms of gameplay is  tricks and it's going to sound a bit  confusing in the beginning hopefully i  can kind of explain it a bit better also  be using the xbox and playstation  buttons on screen just so there's a  visual example so you have three preset  options for tricks racer uses the  buttons trickster uses the right  thumbstick and the third uses the  triggers and bumpers apparently this is  the same control scheme for steep so if  you play steep this might be a bit  easier to use they all pretty much  function the same way pressing a is like  flicking the thumbstick down and  pressing b is like flicking it to the  right for those buttons x and b are for  spins b goes to the right x goes to the  left y and a are for the flips y is a  front flip and a is a backflip you can  however also use the triggers in racer  and trickster mode to do grabs there's a  left grab only using the left trigger  right grabs are for only using the right  trigger and a double grab is when you  use both you can obviously do something  more than just grabbing the board as if  you flick the thumbstick in any  direction that produces a varying amount  of different moves all this together can  look really cool and allow you to pull  off some crazy tricks but the question  is how do you start one in games like  skate 3 a method grab is used by pulling  the right trigger and pushing the left  thumbstick in a specific direction but  to get any air off the jump you need to  do an ollie or kickflip to actually get  the air for it so what's that button for  rider's republic  it's all the face buttons  yeah the buttons used for the tricks are  also the ones for the jumps  i figured this out in the main hub when  i first played as i pressed every single  button on flat ground and i jumped after  everyone like i said it's gonna get a  little confusing so to hopefully explain  a bit better let me go over a front flip  the game does a decent job of explaining  that you press and hold one button when  approaching the ramp then press and hold  another when you get to the top of the  ramp to perform the trick so to do a  front flip you could do y then y again  that's a front flip but you can also do  a then y to do a front flip so now  you're starting to think that as long as  the last button is y it's a front flip  that would be wrong that's only if the  buttons are similar remember a and y are  the only ones that do flips so  technically this works but if you work  in buttons like x or b you'll do a  hybrid trick of both a spin and a flip  to make this a little easier let me show  you step by step a trick i pulled off  incorporating all of this for a more  visual example so to start the race i  hold the right trigger as with any  racing game left trigger is break right  trigger is accelerate as i approach the  ramp i hold the a button to start the  first part i could then press y into a  front flip but instead as we approach  the top of the ramp i let go of the  right trigger and a this means my  character starts the jump we then very  quickly will press down both the  triggers and x and since we went from a  to x we're doing both a spin and a flip  and then since we're using both triggers  we do a double grab at the same time  turning into this  this is just one move or one combination  of buttons and it sounds really  confusing but you'll actually benefit  from doing this yourself rather than  someone explaining it like me because it  becomes muscle memory quite fast there  is a way to help you with tricks at the  main hub which is the trick academy you  simply just select whatever trick you  want to do and the game gives you a  large course and enough room to perform  them at least a dozen times so after all  of this what trick system do you use if  it wasn't obvious by the buttons used  throughout this demonstration i would  say the racer both of them have  incredibly annoying problems with them  but the trickster is by far the worst at  least in my opinion a mechanic i have  yet to bring up is backtracking should  you fall or miss a checkpoint you can  use backtrack which transports you back  a few seconds this doesn't reset time  for everyone else only moves you back a  bit so everyone is still racing at  normal speed the problem with racer is  that the buttons to continue after  backtracking is the a button the same as  the trick button and if you're in the  heat of the race and press it too many  times you might press a to jump causing  you to miss once again this is the same  but arguably worse if you've fallen off  your bike since you have to mash b to  get back on and once again that's a  trick button so there's been numerous  times where i've lost races because of  this however because tricksters use the  right thumbstick for their tricks their  camera is permanently locked to the  position of the racer so you cannot move  the camera and seeing as the fov in this  game is extremely low with no option to  increase it on console you're really  screwing yourself over but that's just  my opinion overall there's lots of depth  to these tricks as there are some simple  tricks like a front flip but then  there's the ones as complicated as the  example i gave it's this loop of getting  the hang of a new trick then attempting  new ones before those become muscle  memory then you move on to the next one  until most of the trick list is  ingrained into you the very last thing  we haven't touched in this video is free  roam mode if you want to race down hills  by yourself or just fly throughout the  map you can do that the map of rider's  republic is quite large and has various  areas in the game for all sports there's  the snowy mountains in the west a dry  desert area in the southeast and even a  forest area dead middle of the map  throughout it though you'll be seeing  loads of icons anything in white as an  ai and anything in orange is a real  player this edition is great and it  makes it feel like a real festival with  thousands of people who all came to ride  together so it's nice to be able to see  other people and even the ais doing  tricks alongside you the place you'll be  visiting the most is the rider's ridge  the home base of the game it has  everything you'll need in regards to  matchmaking training and customization  we've discussed most of this already  throughout the video but as for the only  place we haven't mentioned yet there is  a store that is basically the in-game  item shop these function very similar to  any game that's added in item shop in  the recent years and obviously the good  stuff will be locked behind a paywall  personally i've never liked item shops  but i thought i'd mention it anyway so  you know what you're getting into during  the game after about a one hour tutorial  you are then eventually encouraged to  explore parts of the map the map is  greyed out for the most part and needs  to be unlocked by traveling through the  map this is a very typical formula of  ubisoft's other games like far cry and  assassin's creed and just like those  games leave it to ubisoft to add  collectibles in a game like this there  are 45 landmarks 23 stunts 11 relics and  a whopping 500 balloons the landmarks  were cool after i found out that most if  not all of them are actual real  locations which is real nice and the  stunts are pretty nice too they do  provide a bit more challenge than your  typical events already present in the  game for example the first one is  actually at the main hub it's this very  hard obstacle course that needs to be  beaten in three minutes the relics are  weird equipment called funkies i haven't  mentioned this but there is a group of  riders called the shack daddy bandits  they're famous for doing bizarre stunts  with very weird equipment like wing  suiting with a cardboard plane or using  skis made of old wood they also have  their own events and challenges but  regardless they are pretty cool it's  nice to see some bizarre and wacky  equipment you can use in the game  however the 500 balloons are insane  they're not marked on the map and  they'll only give you a small bit of  money you could claim that it's for the  seasoned player who wants to explore  every inch of the map but i think that's  a poor excuse in an attempt to defend  them ubisoft really wants to get away  from their tried and true formula with  this game but not too far apparently as  they need to add some sort of  collectible with an astronomical number  it's like the borgia flags of assassin's  creed it's just flat out boring content  and not worth your time besides the  collectibles you have a large map that  you can ride and traverse but i'm  confused about what this game wants if  this game was mainly focused on  completing events and getting stars then  they succeeded but then it feels like  you're just going through a checklist  and taking off boxes the best part about  any of these games is the ability to  ride for hours you could run the same  course over and over again or just go  down a path and see where it takes you  but if they tried to do that then they  barely succeeded the best places to get  that thrill of riding your snowboard or  bike is the courses the game gives you  they're used in the events so why not  use them in the free roam mode they're  obviously the best places to ride down  this works perfectly with the snow  events as there's a ski lift at the  bottom of most of the courses that'll  take you right back up to the top and  you can continue riding again but for  the bikes there is nothing if you want  to ride the same course you need to go  to the course's start then once you  finish or are satisfied with your run  you have to fast travel to a nearby  location then bike your way over to the  starting line again depending on the  course this fast travel could be  thousands of feet away so you're not  even riding to the course anymore you're  just flying to the course every time you  want to reset i think you can understand  how boring this would get this game  needs some sort of checkpoint system  like how skate 3 implemented where if  you want to reset you can just be sent  back to the checkpoint that you put down  because right now i cannot see myself  constantly teleporting and traveling to  a course which might be longer than the  actual course itself however before you  start the game there is an option called  the zen mode which apparently according  to ubisoft as i asked them through email  is an offline mode for the game so maybe  there's something like that in there or  maybe i'm just trying to cope regardless  the exploration does have some real  potential and certain areas are really  fun to explore but most of the fun i had  was running the same snow course for an  hour straight and while i may be in the  minority when it comes to people  enjoying that kind of gameplay i  definitely can't be alone as an overall  note to writer's republic is a really  good game it has a 60 price tag so you  do with that as you will but from what  i've experienced the game is quite fun  and it's a game i'll definitely be  playing in my spare time whenever i have  limited time before something i can  always just load the game up right  around for 10 to 15 minutes then turn it  off but it's also beneficial for those  who want to play for hours and hours at  a time of course it's always up to you  whether or not you want to buy the game  with that however we have come to the  end of the video let me know what you  think about writer's republic down below  and if you did participate in that trial  week before the game's launch do let me  know what you thought about the game as  well like the video if you enjoyed  subscribe if you're new and as always if  you're a returning viewer i thank you  for coming back  take care everyone  and goodbye  [Music]  you
 

